\section{Subsystem Synthesis Operator}

Define the synthesis operator:
\[
\mathcal{SYN} :
\left(
\mathcal{K}_{1},\ldots,\mathcal{K}_{7}
\right)
\mapsto
\mathcal{K}_{\text{TSSE-UEK}},
\]
where $\mathcal{K}_{i}$ are subsystem kernels.

\begin{definition}[TSSE Synthesis Operator]
The TSSE synthesis operator combines subsystem kernels into a unified engine kernel.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Synthesis Operator Linearity]
The synthesis operator is linear:
\[
\mathcal{SYN}(\mathcal{K}_{i} + \mathcal{K}_{j})
=
\mathcal{SYN}(\mathcal{K}_{i}) + \mathcal{SYN}(\mathcal{K}_{j}).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Kernel composition corresponds to Hamiltonian addition and manifold product, both
of which are linear operations.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unified Kernel Construction]
The synthesis operator constructs a TSSE-admissible unified engine kernel.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Follows from subsystem admissibility and synthesis operator linearity.
\end{proof}

This theorem establishes the global kernel construction mechanism.

